    "turn_text": "That's something we got. We got to turn to Mexico for our beer. So so anyway, going back to the point that I was making a public policy. So really, the problem becomes when two things happen and they're interrelated. The first is that the people that are in charge of things are completely out of touch with everybody else. Like their lives, their challenges, what they're facing is like everything's going great. I don't know what you guys are talking about. That's challenge number one. And then challenge number two is when their interests are in direct conflict with the interest of everybody else. I think both of those have played out. The first one has always been the case. Maybe not as big, right? I mean, the CEO of a corporation 50 years ago lived a different life than the line worker, but not as different. And I think part of that has to do with the way our economy has changed when we were a country that made things, and that the core of our economy, there was a lot more synergy there. Now we're basically an economy built on finance and services. And so when you're just moving people's money around, it's not the same. You're not managing Employees in the same way would have you so that's different and then on the public policy front and the public policy front in some ways is at the core of what I argue and I say this is someone who has kind of worked through it because I am a I was politically raised. Obviously in the 80s under Reagan, but that was actually different post-Cold War, right? I'm in college in 1991, the Berlin Wall Falls and where everybody's telling us from, you know, all of the journals and the center right beyond is the history is over. And everyone's going to be a democracy. Everyone's going to be a free enterprise economy. And from now on, nationhood is not going to matter as much because what's going to link us and interconnect us as humans is that we're all consumers and we're all investors and we're going to be making too much money off each other to go to war and things of that nature. So that was the gist of the argument. And so that's created a set of public policies that have really worked well for people that are in that realm and that are in that world where they move money around the world where they benefit from globalization that benefit from borderless and world. But it's hurt millions and millions of people who are left behind and were told don't worry yes your job is going away but a better job is going to come and that better job is going to pay more and that better job number materialized and now That has not just economic impacts, right? But it destroys communities and all of the institutions that support it. You can't have little leagues, can have PTAs, you can have churches and civic organizations, you can have, I mean, how many hollowed out communities do we, you can come up with a list of 15 or 20 flint Michigan. These are, these were prosperous places 25 years ago that have been hollowed out and everything that comes with it. So I think that's really the disconnect and the biggest, and to me, how I learned that lesson most powerfully, right? I'm not calling anybody evil here. I'm grateful for their help, but I would go out and do town halls in New Hampshire. And then I would do a fundraiser in Boston or New York and it's two different planets. It's like their priorities were different. And so I realized that even within the Republican party, there was a voter base of people that were felt increasing left behind. And then the donor base, that basically felt like stop talking about social and cultural issues and let's focus on cutting taxes and regulations and helping what we have now grow even faster. So what we have to do about it is recognize that that's the problem we have and that's a tough thing to do because we are increasingly isolated from you can live your entire existence and a bubble of people that all agree with you that kids should be taught about gender when they're seven years old in school and anybody who doesn't Believes that, for examples, on knuckle-dragger and backwards. That's how you can live your whole life in these bubbles. Washington, in some ways, is that kind of bubble. Certainly some of the other areas aren't. And it sort of increasingly isolates us from each other. And all it does is feed us, you know, the things that confirm our biases already. So",
