    "turn_text": "Well, look, I don't think that TikTok has survived anything. I think what they've survived is that Congress doesn't move quickly on anything to begin with. And so this is no less the case. I also think what it has been able to do is find voices within the political class that are arguing on their behalf for a lot of different reasons. It's also a tough issue. Look, it's hard to go to people and say there's something you really like and it's a lot of fun and are you enjoyed using it and we're going to take it away from you and you just have to trust this. It's really bad for the country. So you've got to make a compelling argument about why and it requires a little bit of being able to see into the future. It's not so much what TikTok is doing now is how the algorithm that's controlled out of China and always will be. By Chinese law that algorithm can never leave China, no matter who owns TikTok. It doesn't work without that algorithm and by dance will always own it. Explaining to people how that can be used against us, but it's tough because people like it and I guess the The sort of simpler analogy to it would be you know telling people I know you really like that those cheap clothes that you're getting $13 on she know whatever online We're gonna take it away from them. They're like well why can't we just I don't like China I'm afraid of China, but I do still want to buy the $12 shirts that cost 50 somewhere else and It's hard to take that away from people. So I think that they, what has happened basically is that Congress just takes a long time and it just takes a handful of people to slow things down.",
