  {
    "episode_id": 95576,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95576.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 70,
    "turn_text": "So let me ask you a question about that because you and so many others who are in your generation of politicians have had to sort of navigate a difficult series of challenges when it comes to foreign policy. Namely that you and no one really at your age range or below was actually a decision maker when the neoconservative foreign policy group was in charge. You were the people who were under them and under them and under them by a number of different levels, trying to make that policy work, et cetera. You were not a decision maker at that point. And so when I hear a lot of people getting slammed with the neo-con label right now in 2023. I'm like, what are you talking about? They didn't have any power to change American foreign policy, you know, at that time. And yet there's also this kind of conflict over what America first foreign policy actually means. And you can get a different definition depending on who you ask. If you ask Bill Haggerty, it's going to be a different definition than you ask, you know, people who are more closely aligned with President, former President Trump. If you ask, you know, you're sort of paleo cons, they'll describe anybody to their to, you know, in a distance from them as being neo con as just kind of a slight. How do you define the foreign policy of a responsible, pro America, Republican coalition going forward,",
