    "turn_text": "In decades of decadence, you write about the dangers of the multinational firms that are weighing into politics in an increasingly aggressive way. You write about this, particularly in the wake of the Dobbs decision and focus on the abortion side of things. I wonder what your attitude is toward that. I mean, you came up through a brand of conservatism coming out of the Reagan era, as you said before, that was essentially pro-capitalism, but also pro-business, and basically said, Assumed that businesses would be prioritizing consumers of all political stripes. Now they are taking firm stands often in some ways, just for the papers or for the news media, but saying we don't want to be active in certain states because of their policies, virtue signaling in different ways, weighing in on policies in ways that obviously have created uh, tension between those business communities and a lot of conservative politicians. What's your take on the proper approach to things like that? Um, you know, from a policymaker perspective, it's one thing to just sort of say, Hey, people can, you know, boycott whoever they want or, you know, buy or not buy whatever product they want, but from a policymaker's perspective, how do you approach it?",
