    "turn_text": "a couple of things. First of all, just, We have to know, just because a company has an address in the United States of America does not mean that they are an American company, even though they're protected by our laws, they are operating in what they believe to be either the best interest of their company or the demand of their institutional shareholders and their executives. So in the case of institutional shareholders, who are they? Where are there people from all over the world? but there are also these large firms who basically go in and say we don't care target losing a bunch of money we want you to do this because we own forty fifty percent of shares and we both those shares even though we're not individual owners we the investment firm control your company and you see that black rock and bangard so forth that control huge chunks. of the shares in these companies, and then who populates the work? Well, so we've had a higher education system that for 25 or 30 years has been pumping, has been indoctrinating in every field, particularly in the liberal arts and in the business schools, in the notions that America's terrible, America's evil or society's broken. You know the job of life is to be active this on all these issues. And then these people graduate and they go into the workplace that that's who's populated in the boardrooms and all so it's not surprising what happened with Bud Light we joked about that a moment ago but this is someone a product of our university system. They put them in charge of marketing they came up with an idea that I'm sure would have been very very popular on whatever campus. This woman graduated from, but you know, ran into the reality that there's a huge swaths of America to think it's not and almost has done tremendous damage to that corporate brand. Likewise with Target, you know, I don't look, I don't care if men want to go and shop in the women's section of target what business is that of mine i i i i have a viewer that's a lot of interesting but i mean that's free country but now",
