    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95576.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 105,
    "turn_text": "So I don't often promote to you all the work that I do on my sub stack, the transom, which you can find at thetransom.com. You can go there and subscribe. both for free and there's a paid subscription as well. But I wanted to just let you all know that we have a book club that has restarted now as part of this sub stack. It's something that is designed to be a way for a lot of our different readers and listeners to gather together and discuss books of interest. And it's something that I've used to delve into a lot of different areas of American life and literature. Particularly, we have spent a lot of time on sort of modern media criticism books by David Mamet looking at Neil Postman's, Amusing Ourselves to Death and the Like. Our latest book, one that I think would be of interest to probably a lot of you, is Empires of the Sea by a British historian, Roger Crowley, who is looking back at particularly the kind of 50-year period in the middle of the 16th century when you saw so many different significant battles take place in the sea of the Mediterranean. It's one of these moments in history in which you had so many different significant historical factions clash in ways that are of particular interest. And so you go from the siege of Malta to the Battle of Lepanto and you learn all these different things that kind of flow out of that into the world that we know today. This is an underappreciated aspect and period of world history and one that I think is particularly worth studying. And Roger Crowley's book is one of the best in terms of summarizing and getting to the core of the historical elements that were going on there in a way that isn't just, you know, sort of dry academic work but is very gripping and interesting. If you're interested in participating in the book club, you can join it, as I said, by signing up for the transom. We have our regular Zoom that people participate in once a month, and we'll be doing another book next month if you don't have the time to do it this month. But I hope that you'll consider joining up because we really do have a great time discussing some of these works. And especially post-pandemic, I feel like it's important to get back to the idea of discussing different books together, having folks gather together to discuss these types of things, too much of our lives, I think, has lived separate nowadays, and it's good to be able to come together and have a conversation about things that are of interest and of great importance. I'm Ben Damanette, you've been listening to another edition of the Ben Damanette podcast. We'll be back soon with more to dive back into the fray. Listen ad-free with a Fox News Podcast Plus subscription on Apple Podcasts. And Amazon Prime members can listen to this show ad-free on the Amazon Music Cast. from the Fox News Podcasts Network.",
